<?xml version="1.0" encoding="utf-8"?>
<network-security-config>
  <!--
    Cleartext policy.

    The app talks to exactly two kinds of endpoint:

      1. LOOPBACK — its own credential bridge. The WebView loads
         127.0.0.1, where AuthProxy adds the bearer token and the
         per-request device signature. This traffic never leaves the phone.

      2. THE PAIRED GATE, over the tailnet. Every byte of that hop is
         already encrypted end to end by WireGuard, and the gate's /api
         routes additionally demand the bearer token plus a live device
         signature. TLS on top would add nothing an attacker could not
         already bypass by being outside the tailnet entirely.

    Android's network-security-config matches literal hostnames, not CIDR
    ranges, so "the tailnet" cannot be expressed as a rule. Enumerating the
    100.64/10 space would mean 64 entries, and naming the user's own host
    would leak their network into the APK — the exact leak we removed.

    So cleartext is permitted by default and the REAL protection is the one
    that actually holds: the gate refuses anything without the token and a
    fresh device signature, and it is unreachable outside the tailnet.
  -->
  <base-config cleartextTrafficPermitted="true"/>
</network-security-config>
